<p>Two days of work, one day of maintenance. Day 3 is a stretching sequence, not a workout: four holds, a 20-count each, done on both sides where the position has a side, then the whole sequence repeated once more. The poses on the card are a standing quad stretch, a standing hip and glute stretch over a crossed leg, an overhead triceps stretch and a chest opener with the hands clasped behind the back - the exact muscles Days 1 and 2 just used.</p>
<p>The structure is simple but specific. A 20-count is long enough for the stretched muscle to stop guarding and actually lengthen - short holds only tease the nervous system, longer ones persuade it. Doing each side separately matters just as much: one quad or one hip will almost always feel tighter than the other, and that asymmetry is useful information about how your body is absorbing the program. Running the full sequence twice means the second pass always goes a little deeper than the first, because the tissue is already warm and the guarding reflex has already relaxed.</p>
<p>In the quad stretch, keep your knees together and push your hips slightly forward rather than yanking the foot. In the hip stretch, sit back into the crossed-leg position until you feel it in the outer hip, not the knee. For the triceps stretch, reach your hand down your spine and use the other hand to gently guide the elbow. In the chest opener, clasp your hands behind you, straighten your arms and lift them slightly while keeping your ribs down.</p>
<p>The program continues tomorrow - today your job is to arrive there loose.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Harder:</strong> Wrong goal for a rest day - if you want more, count slower and let each hold run longer instead.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Easier:</strong> Do the sequence once through instead of twice.</p>
