<p>Day 5 pairs your core with its other half. The session is straight sets: 20 bird dogs, 20 dead bugs and 12 reverse angels, each exercise done for all three sets with 60 seconds of rest between everything. No crunches anywhere in sight - this is core training in the anti-movement sense, where the muscles work to keep your spine still while your limbs try to disturb it.</p>
<p>Bird dogs and dead bugs are the same exercise mirrored. Face down on all fours, the bird dog extends opposite arm and leg while your back stays flat; face up, the dead bug lowers opposite arm and leg while your lower back stays pressed into the floor. One trains stability against gravity from above, the other from below, and doing both back to back covers the trunk in full. Reverse angels then close the loop on the back of the shoulders: lying face down, sweeping the arms from overhead to your sides forces the lower traps and rear delts to work against gravity through their entire range - twelve reps of the postural muscles a desk quietly switches off.</p>
<p>In the bird dog, the goal is a motionless torso: imagine balancing a cup of water on your lower back while the arm and leg reach away from each other. In the dead bug, exhale as the limbs lower and stop the moment your lower back tries to peel off the floor - that is your range for today. In reverse angels, keep the arms hovering off the ground for the entire sweep and lead the movement from between your shoulder blades.</p>
<p>Nothing here looks hard from the outside - your spine will file a different report.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Harder:</strong> Slow every rep to a three-count reach and a three-count return - the slower the limbs, the harder the trunk works.</p>
