<p>Day 7 is a rest day that borrows the format of a work day. Three isometric holds - a 30-count standing chest opener with hands clasped behind your back, a 30-count squat hold and a 20-count chest squeeze - each done for 3 sets with the program's standard 60 seconds of rest in between. Unlike the stretching sequence of Day 3, this rest day asks your muscles to do something: hold a position, quietly, until the count runs out.</p>
<p>Each hold has its own agenda. The chest opener is a stretch disguised as a stance - clasped hands and straight arms pull the shoulders back and open the front of the chest, undoing a week of arms working in front of the body. The squat hold sits your legs at half depth and leaves them there, building tension the joints barely feel: no reps, no impact, just muscles idling under load. The chest squeeze you already know from Day 2, and here it keeps the upper body honest without adding any movement to recover from. Low intensity held for time is the theme - effort without wear.</p>
<p>In the chest opener, lift the clasped hands slightly away from your body and keep your chin level. In the squat hold, thighs approach parallel, weight in your heels, back straight - find the depth you can hold for the full count without shaking apart in the first ten. In the chest squeeze, press firmly and keep your breathing slow and steady; the count only counts if the pressure never drops.</p>
<p>One week of Primer done - today you hold still and let the adaptation catch up with you.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Harder:</strong> That is not what a rest day is for - if the holds feel easy, count slower and breathe deeper instead.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Easier:</strong> Raise the squat hold a few inches - a quarter squat held for the full count beats a deep one you abandon early.</p>
