<p>Day 13 is the standing session, expanded. Straight sets at 4 sets each, 60 seconds rest: 20 reverse lunges, 20 bicep extensions, 20 front leg raises and 20 arm circles. Like Day 6, nothing touches the floor - but where that session borrowed three exercises, this one runs four, alternating lower and upper body so no region works twice in a row.</p>
<p>The alternating order is the recovery plan built into the sequence. Lunges load the legs, bicep extensions give them a full exercise plus rest to recover while the arms curl against their own tension, front leg raises return to the legs, and arm circles close the session upstairs. The two arm exercises are a study in contrast: bicep extensions are deliberate, squeezing the arm through a curl with no weight but full intent, while arm circles are endurance in disguise - twenty rotations with arms parallel to the floor, where the deltoids discover that holding the position is the real exercise and the circles are just decoration.</p>
<p>Lunge form does not change with familiarity: back knee low, front heel doing the work. In bicep extensions, clench your fists and tense the arm through both directions - self-resistance only works if you supply the resistance. Front leg raises stay controlled to hip height with a straight knee. In arm circles, keep the circles small and the arms dead level; size is not the point, time at shoulder height is.</p>
<p>Two weeks in, the full body session has four movements instead of three - the roll call is getting longer.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Harder:</strong> Reverse the arm circle direction halfway through each set without lowering your arms.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Easier:</strong> Cut arm circles and bicep extensions to 12 reps per set and keep both leg exercises as written.</p>
