<p>The last stretch day of the program reaches for the whole back line. Four standing positions, each held for a 20-count per side where sides apply, then the full sequence repeated: a standing forward fold - with the card's own note to go as low as you can - a standing quad stretch, the overhead triceps stretch and a standing side bend. Legs, hips, arms and trunk, all serviced in one unhurried tour.</p>
<p>The forward fold earns its special instruction. Three weeks of forward bends have been loading your hamstrings; this is where you find out what they got in return. "As low as you can" is not a challenge to force depth - it is an invitation to see where the floor is today, hang there, and let gravity and the 20-count do the lengthening. The quad stretch balances the fold by hitting the front of the thigh, the triceps stretch keeps the week's arm work from tightening overhead range, and the side bend closes with the trunk's flanks - the one line of the body the program's straight-ahead exercises never quite reach. Two passes, as always: the first opens the door, the second walks through it.</p>
<p>In the fold, let the head hang heavy and the knees soften - reaching the floor with a bent spine teaches nothing, hanging with a long one teaches plenty. Quad stretch: knees together, hips pressed gently forward. Triceps: elbow guided, not yanked. In the side bend, lean directly sideways - no twist, no forward collapse - and let the stretch travel from hip to armpit.</p>
<p>Four weeks of bends built this fold - today you get to measure it in centimetres.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Harder:</strong> Stretching does not have a harder gear - it has a deeper one, and the card already told you where to find it.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Easier:</strong> One pass instead of two, and bend the knees as generously as the fold needs.</p>
