<p>Day 28 is not a workout - it is a measurement. Three positions you know intimately by now: the squat hold, the standing hold with hands clasped behind your back, and the chest squeeze. No counts this time, no sets. Hold each one for as long as you can, rest 60 seconds, move to the next. The program's final page is a test, and the test is simply: how long?</p>
<p>The choice of exercises is the point. These are the three holds the rest days have been progressing all month - 30 counts, then 40, then 45 - which means you arrive at this benchmark with a month of data behind you and a body that has practiced exactly this. Max-duration holds are the cleanest self-test in bodyweight training: no reps to miscount, no tempo to fudge, just a position and a clock and the moment your form finally gives. Write the three numbers down. They are your baseline - the honest, dated record of where four weeks of Primer left you, and the target the next program gets to chase.</p>
<p>The hold ends when the form ends, not when it gets uncomfortable: the squat hold is over when your thighs rise above your chosen depth, the standing hold when your shoulders slump forward, the squeeze when the pressure drops. Breathe throughout all three - a hold you breathe through lasts twice as long as one you brace against. Count in your head or use a timer, but count something: a benchmark without a number is just a Tuesday.</p>
<p>Four weeks ago this program introduced itself gently - today you hand it three numbers that prove you were paying attention.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Harder:</strong> A benchmark scales itself - the harder version is simply the longer hold, and that is precisely what you are here to find.</p>
<p><strong>Make it Easier:</strong> Take the holds on separate rests of two minutes instead of one - the test measures your holds, not your recovery.</p>
